## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

