## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying a simple bitwise identity (Gray code) across a non-native 128-bit representation, including cross-boundary shifts. It enforces branch-free, constant-time thinking and careful hi/lo handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using 64-bit halves
- Gray code relation: G = X ^ (X >> 1)
- Cross-boundary shifts between hi and lo

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 toGray128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hiGray = x.hi ^ (x.hi >> 1);
  uint64_t loGray = x.lo ^ ((x.lo >> 1) | (x.hi << 63));
  return (uint128){.hi = hiGray, .lo = loGray};
}
```

